ck":"Vahe A. Sukiasyan","labels":["book","book","book","book","book","book","book","book","book","book","book","best","best","best","excellent","good","best","good","best","excellent","excellent","excellent","best","best","best","best","best","best","best","best","excellent","excellent","excellent","good","good","best","good","excellent","good","good","best","best","good","excellent","good","best","best","best","excellent","best","best","best","great","best","best","best","best","best","inaccuracy","best","excellent","good","inaccuracy","excellent","best","best","excellent","great","best","best","best","best","good","best","best","best","excellent","best","good","good","inaccuracy","best","best","great","best","best","inaccuracy","brilliant"],"moments":[{"ply":53,"name":"27.f4","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"27.f4! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Attacks the knight on e5.","Pushes a pawn towards the Black king, to pry open lines in front of it.","The next best move, 27.exd5, would drop White's winning chances from 45% to 27% (−2.70).","27.f4 threatens fxe5, winning a knight.","It threatens fxe5, winning a knight, so Black has to spend the next move on it.","Black answers 27…Nc6, opening the line of the queen on b8 to the f4 pawn."]},{"ply":68,"name":"34…Bxg2+","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"34…Bxg2+! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Takes the bishop on g2.","The next best move, 34…Bf8, would drop Black's winning chances from 65% to 54% (−0.45).","34…Bxg2+ trades bishops and gives check, leaving two replies.","Trades bishops.","Check, and White has only two legal replies.","White answers 35.Kxg2, taking back, leaving Black with no bishop pair."]},{"ply":84,"name":"42…Rxd3","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"42…Rxd3! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Takes the rook on d3.","The next best move, 42…Ne3, would drop Black's winning chances from 85% to 73% (−2.76).","42…Rxd3 wins a pawn and trades off the attacked rook instead of losing it to Bxc3.","Takes the rook on d3 and wins a pawn once the captures settle.","White was threatening Bxc3, winning a rook for a piece and a pawn. The rook takes something with it instead.","White answers 43.Rxd3, taking back."]},{"ply":88,"name":"44…Nf2+","cls":"brilliant","color":"b","headline":"44…Nf2+!! A real sacrifice: Black gives up material and the engine still rates it best.","points":["Puts the knight on f2 where it can be taken.","White can take, but after 45.Qxf2 Qh1+ 46.Qh2 Qf1+ the engine rates it −6.55.","Check, and White has only two legal replies.","44…Nf2+ gives check, leaving two replies.","White answers 45.Qxf2, winning a knight.","After 45.Qxf2 Qh1+ 46.Qh2 Qf1+ 47.Qg2 Qxd3 48.Qf3 Qxf3 49.Nxf3 Rc2, the queens come off and Black comes out two pawns up (−6.55)."],"why":[{"title":"The idea","text":"44…Nf2+!! offers the knight on f2. Taking it gives Black the material back and more."},{"title":"What Black gives up","text":"44…Nf2+ puts the knight on f2 where it can be taken by the queen on g2. That's a knight on offer."},{"title":"If White takes the knight","text":"45.Qxf2 is met by 45…Qh1+. Then 46…Qf1+ gives check, and 47…Qxd3 takes the rook on d3. Black ends up a piece and a pawn ahead: Black is winning (−5.55). This is White's best try."},{"title":"Without the sacrifice","text":"The best move without the sacrifice, 44…Qa2, is clearly weaker (−4.96 against −6.55). White answers 45.Rb3, threatening Rxb4, winning a bishop and a pawn. Black would still be winning, but far less than after 44…Nf2+."}]}],"opening":{"name":"English Opening: Agincourt Defense","eco":"A13","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 6.Nc3: 11 moves in all, 6 by White and 5 by Black.","Theory runs out after 6.Nc3: too few games between strong players reached this position to go further."]}}